WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.501
[music playing]

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:02.802
The coastline,
extending roughly

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:09.342
15 miles around the town,
is a shark-attack hot spot.

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:11.077
There have been as
many as 20 shark attacks

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:13.847
in a single year, which
is a tremendous number

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:16.082
for such a small area.

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:19.519
NARRATOR: In fact,
since records began,

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:22.322
this tiny region has
seen a shocking total

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:24.691
of more than 220 shark attacks.

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:31.164
That means roughly one
out of every 25 attacks

00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:36.636
worldwide happens
here, earning the area

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.739
A terrifying claim to fame.

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:42.442
New Smyrna Beach has
been nicknamed the Shark

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.878
Attack Capital of the World.

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:47.981
NARRATOR: It's a reputation
no one in this tourist haven

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:49.282
wants.

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:52.652
People ask me every day,
are there sharks out there?

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.454
We really can't tell
what the cause is

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.923
when people get bit out there.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:00.360
A lot of people will not
come here because of that.

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:03.029
NARRATOR: This
problem must be solved

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:08.768
for the sake of beachgoers
here and across the nation.

00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:10.804
It's really important to
identify the factors that are

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.739
associated with the
spike in shark attacks,

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:14.007
because we have
a chance of using

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:15.408
that knowledge to
keep beachgoers

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:18.278
safe throughout the world.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:22.048
NARRATOR: So what makes
New Smyrna so different?

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:26.519
Why are humans more likely to be
bitten here than anywhere else

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:29.422
on Earth?

00:01:29.489 --> 00:01:31.991
Perhaps a clue can be
found within the species

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:35.962
responsible for Emma's incident.

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:40.066
The vast majority of attacks
worldwide are conducted by one

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.602
of three types of sharks--

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:49.509
bulls, tigers, or whites.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:52.745
But based on the pattern
of Emma's wounds,

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:57.283
experts believe she might have
been bitten by a blacktip.

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:03.456
Blacktip sharks are found
in warm, coastal waters

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:05.492
across the globe.

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:08.962
They often feed in
large schools and are

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:11.731
easily recognizable by
the black-colored markings

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.833
on their fins.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.136
And they grow up to
8 feet long, which

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:20.740
means they're large enough
to take down a human.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.679
Dr. Stephen Kajiura
has been studying

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:29.916
blacktips along Florida's
coast for almost a decade.

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:38.024
Each year, he takes to the
skies to research this species,

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:40.760
taking part in one of the
most awe-inspiring marine

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:43.863
phenomenons in the world.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.299
This footage shows
what we've captured

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.334
by doing an aerial survey.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.469
You can see all
those little dots.

00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:52.872
Every one of those is a shark.

00:02:52.939 --> 00:02:55.608
Just in that narrow band,
we're seeing literally

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:59.212
tens of thousands of sharks.

00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:03.550
NARRATOR: This is the blacktip
migration, the largest shark

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:06.486
migration in the United States.

00:03:06.553 --> 00:03:07.620
There are hundreds
of thousands

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:09.489
of these blacktips
making this migration

00:03:09.556 --> 00:03:11.424
along the coast every year.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:12.792
They come down to
spend the winter

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:17.230
here in Florida, because the
temperature is ideal for them.

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:18.932
NARRATOR: Many different
types of sharks

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:26.005
migrate annually in search of
food, warmer waters, or a mate.

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:29.108
Most travel alone,
or in small groups.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:32.979
And they typically
do so in deep waters.

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:35.448
But the potential
problem for beachgoers

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:41.588
is that the hordes of
blacktips prefer the shallows.

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:44.023
STEPHEN KAJIURA: These
sharks hug the shoreline.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.592
They might be doing
that to stay away

00:03:45.658 --> 00:03:47.694
from predators-- baiter sharks.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.029
So we have thousands
of them so close

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:53.766
to shore, right along the beach
where all the bathers are.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:55.535
NARRATOR: Any time
there is an influx

00:03:55.602 --> 00:03:59.505
of sharks in an area, the
chance of a run-in with humans

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:00.540
also increases.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:08.414
So is the large number of
attacks near New Smyrna simply

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:11.351
the result of this large
number of blacktips inshore?

00:04:14.721 --> 00:04:18.925
The answer comes down to timing.

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:23.730
The blacktips pass New Smyrna
from September to November,

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:27.100
then again in March and April.

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:30.570
And only about half of
the attacks on record

00:04:30.637 --> 00:04:34.240
took place during those months.

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:40.747
So the migration may play a
part in some of the incidents,

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:41.814
but it's not the whole story.

